          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong Cartesian space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+product"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            with itself. Hence, a Cartesian space has the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            (possibly
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
          %p
            What exactly this means depends on which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is being considered as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of.
          %p
            For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            (hence an object in the category
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifold"} differentiable manifold
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            (hence an object in the categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TopMfd"} TopMfd
            or
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is the archetypical such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+manifold"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (the basic
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
          %p
            The Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            with its standard
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            is also called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                real
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            One could also regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real vector space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            1, in which case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            would be the real vector space of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (maybe understood as equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            canonically induced by this presentation), hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            “real
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            But in between the last two examples, regarding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            as an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            makes it the basis of “
            %a{:href => "https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analytic_geometry"}> analytic geometry
            ” in the sense originally due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ren%C3%A9+Descartes"} René Descartes
            (
            %span.newWikiWord>
              Cartesian geometry
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Cartesian+geometry"} ?
            ), and this is where the term “Cartesian space” originates from.
          %p
            One might also speak of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexified"} complexified
            cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^n
            for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+space"} analytic space
          .un_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p In particular:
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^0
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
                is the real
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex plane
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p Cartesian spaces carry plenty of further canonical structure:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  It is canonically a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
                  and the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+topology"} Euclidean topology
                  is the corresponding
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric topology
              %li
                %p
                  There is a canonical
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
                  on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
                  that makes it a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %li
                %p
                  A Cartesian space is canonically a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                  over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                  of
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %p
              Sometimes one is interested in allowing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              to take other values, in which case one wants a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
              in some category that might not be the Cartesian product on underlying sets.
            %p
              For example, if one is studying Cartesian spaces as
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner product space
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_0
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_0
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi l
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^2
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ℵ
                            %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{\aleph_0}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#topological_structure Topological structure
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+n-ball"} open n-ball
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔹
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{B}^n \simeq \mathbb{R}^n
                  \\,.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
          %h3#smooth_structures Smooth structures
          %p
            For all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+n-ball"} open n-ball
            with its standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphic"} diffeomorphic
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            with its standard smooth structure
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔹
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{B}^n \simeq \mathbb{R}^n
                  \\,.
          %p
            In fact, in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \neq 4
            there is no choice:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \neq 4
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            This was shown in (
            %a{:href => "#Stallings"}> Stallings
            ).
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              In
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
              the analog of this statement is false. One says that on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
              there exist
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+smooth+structure"} exotic smooth structure
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \mathbb{N}
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \neq 4
              we have:
            %p
              every open subset of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^d
              is also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphic"} diffeomorphic
              to it.
          %p
            See the first page of (
            %a{:href => "#Ozols"}> Ozols
            ) for a list of references.
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In dimension 4 the analog statement fails due to the existence of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exotic+smooth+structure"} exotic smooth structure
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
          %h2#the_category_of_cartesian_spaces The category of Cartesian spaces
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartesian+space"} super Cartesian space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polydisk"} polydisk
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
            for purposes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cohomology
            the role of Cartesian spaces is played by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+manifolds"} Stein manifolds
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Cartesian spaces are named after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ren%C3%A9+Descartes"} René Descartes
          %blockquote
            %p
              See also references at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/piecewise-linear+manifold"} piecewise-linear manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of Cartesian spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Stallings"}> John Stallings
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Piecewise-Linear Structure of Euclidean Space
              %strong 58
              3 (1962) 481–488 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100036756"}> doi:10.1017/S0305004100036756
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.maths.gla.ac.uk/~mpowell/stallings-structures-of-euclidean-space.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Ozols
              V. Ozols,
              %em Largest normal neighbourhoods
              , Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society Vol. 61, No. 1 (Nov., 1976), pp. 99-101 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/2041672"}> jstor
              )
          %p
            There are various slight variations of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CartSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp
            that one can consider without changing its basic properties as a category of test spaces for
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ThCartSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ThCartSp
            of infinitesimally thickened Cartesian spaces is known as the site for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers+topos"} Cahiers topos
          %p in detal in section 5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              %em Convenient vector spaces embed into the Cahiers topos
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1986__27_1_3_0"}> numdam
              )
          %p and briefly mentioned in example 2) on p. 191 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              %em Synthetic differential geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/sdg99.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p following the original article
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
              ,
              %em Sur les modeles de la geometrie differentielle synthetique
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1979__20_3_231_0"}> numdam
              ).
          %p
            With an eye towards
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+space"} Frölicher space
          %ul
            %li
              Hirokazu Nishimura,
              %em Beyond the Regnant Philosophy of Manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0827"}> arXiv:0912.0827
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
